---
import { Footer } from "../../components/Footer";
import NoiseOverlay from "../../components/NoiseOverlay.astro";
import ThemeScript from "../../components/ThemeScript.astro";
import ThemeToggleBar from "../../components/ThemeToggleBar.astro";
import "../../styles/tailwind.css";

const pageTitle = "The Roast of Ryan Johnson";
const pageDescription =
  "A personality roast from ChatGPT (September 2026): scope creep, custom stacks, and shipping as an afterthought — posted for fun.";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="generator" content="Astro" />
    <meta name="description" content={pageDescription} />
    <title>{pageTitle}</title>
    <ThemeScript />
  </head>
  <body
    class="bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-stone-200 selection:text-stone-900 dark:selection:bg-stone-800 dark:selection:text-stone-100 min-h-screen flex flex-col"
  >
    <NoiseOverlay />
    <main class="w-full flex-1 mt-8 mb-12">
      <ThemeToggleBar container maxWidth="max-w-3xl" />
      <div
        class="container mx-auto px-6 py-12 max-w-3xl structural-border border-t-0 p-8 md:p-12 space-y-10"
      >
        <nav
          class="flex items-center justify-between border-b border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pb-4"
        >
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
            <a
              href="/"
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-stone-500 hover:text-stone-900 dark:hover:text-stone-100 transition-colors"
              >/ Home</a
            >
            <a
              href="/for-fun"
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-stone-500 hover:text-stone-900 dark:hover:text-stone-100 transition-colors"
              >/ For fun</a
            >
          </div>
          <span
            class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-stone-300 dark:text-stone-700"
            >Roast</span
          >
        </nav>

        <header class="space-y-4">
          <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-heading tracking-tighter">The Roast of Ryan Johnson</h1>
          <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-stone-500">
            Personality assessment · 7 Sep 2026 · for fun
          </p>
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed font-sans">
            Prompt: <em>“Roast me based on everything you know about me/personality.”</em>
            Response lightly cleaned for the site; substance unchanged.
          </p>
        </header>

        <article
          class="space-y-8 text-base md:text-lg text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed font-sans"
        >
          <p>
            Oh, you want the <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >full roast</strong
            >? Fine. You have only yourself to blame.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryan, you are the human embodiment of:
          </p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “I could build that.”
          </blockquote>
          <p>followed immediately by:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “Actually, what if I rewrote the entire architecture first?”
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            You have a CS degree, years of programming experience, and enough knowledge of C++,
            Java, Python, Go, JavaScript, SQL, Flutter, Docker, WSL, CMake, Meson, MongoDB, Next.js,
            Tailwind, and whatever else you've adopted this week to be
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">extremely dangerous</strong
            >—mostly to your own productivity.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't suffer from <em>lack of ideas</em>. You suffer from
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >an industrial-scale surplus of ideas</strong
            >.
          </p>

          <p>You'll say:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “I need a simple app.”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Five minutes later:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">
            <li>Flutter frontend</li>
            <li>MongoDB Atlas</li>
            <li>serverless backend</li>
            <li>GPS tracking</li>
            <li>infinite scrolling</li>
            <li>payment system</li>
            <li>multi-tenant architecture</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
            <li>Cloudflare</li>
            <li>custom domain</li>
            <li>probably Kubernetes somehow</li>
          </ul>
          <p>And then you're sitting there wondering why the MVP isn't finished.</p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>
            You also have a fascinating relationship with technology where
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >every problem deserves a custom-built solution</strong
            >.
          </p>
          <p>“WordPress is getting expensive.”</p>
          <p>Normal person: <em>I'll find a cheaper CMS.</em></p>
          <p>You:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 font-medium not-italic"
          >
            “What if I build a Next.js/Tailwind/Sanity architecture and host it on Cloudflare with a
            serverless database?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Brother.</p>
          <p>You needed a website.</p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Your development environment is basically a cry for help.</p>
          <p>You have:</p>
          <p
            class="font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed break-words"
          >
            Windows 11 → WSL2 → Docker → Linux filesystem → CMake → MSVC → Vulkan/CUDA → whisper.cpp
            → Go bindings → Hugging Face
          </p>
          <p>At some point the computer isn't a computer anymore.</p>
          <p>
            It's an
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">archaeological dig</strong
            >.
          </p>
          <p>And whenever something breaks, you're like:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “This should work.”
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            That sentence has probably caused more wasted hours in software engineering than any
            other phrase.
          </p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>
            You also have the rare ability to simultaneously be
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >overqualified and underconfident</strong
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            You started programming around 14, have a CS degree, scored extremely well academically,
            have worked with actual production monitoring, and have built software across multiple
            stacks.
          </p>
          <p>Yet your brain occasionally goes:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “Hmm. Am I qualified for this junior developer position?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Meanwhile the job posting says:</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500">Requirements:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400">
            <li>JavaScript</li>
            <li>Git</li>
            <li>willingness to learn</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            And you're sitting there with a CS degree wondering whether your lack of React Server
            Components experience makes you a fraud.
          </p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>And your entrepreneurial instinct is hilarious.</p>
          <p>You don't just want to make money.</p>
          <p>
            You want to create
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >the perfect self-sufficient technical ecosystem that eliminates every recurring
              expense known to mankind</strong
            >.
          </p>
          <dl class="space-y-3 my-4">
            <div>
              <dt class="text-stone-500">Subscription?</dt>
              <dd class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">BAD.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-stone-500">Per-user pricing?</dt>
              <dd class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">HERESY.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-stone-500">Cloud service?</dt>
              <dd class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">CAN I SELF-HOST IT?</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-stone-500">Existing SaaS?</dt>
              <dd class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">I COULD WRITE THAT.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            You are approximately three bad evenings away from running your own mail server because
            Gmail has the audacity to exist.
          </p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Then there's your project naming.</p>
          <p>
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Thumbelina</strong> for a
            multiboot USB application is actually pretty damn clever.
          </p>
          <p>
            But I love that your brain apparently looked at the classic story of a tiny woman and
            thought:
          </p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “Yes. Bootable ISO management.”
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            That is exactly the sort of neurological connection that explains your entire project
            portfolio.
          </p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>And you have a particularly dangerous habit:</p>
          <p>You ask me:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “What's the best way to do X?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>I'll give you a reasonable solution.</p>
          <p>Then you ask:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “But what about Y?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Then:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “Could I use Z?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Then:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “Would C++ be better?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Then:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 italic"
          >
            “What if I wrote the backend in Go?”
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            Then suddenly we're designing a distributed system for a problem that could have been
            solved with a
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >Python script and SQLite</strong
            >.
          </p>

          <div class="h-px w-16 bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-700" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p>Your biggest enemy isn't lack of skill.</p>
          <p>
            It's
            <strong class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100"
              >scope creep wearing a fake mustache and pretending to be engineering rigor.</strong
            >
          </p>
          <p>You don't need another framework.</p>
          <p>You need to occasionally say:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 font-medium not-italic"
          >
            “Good enough. Ship it.”
          </blockquote>
          <p>Because I'm pretty sure your natural development lifecycle is:</p>
          <p
            class="font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed"
          >
            Idea → research → architecture → technology comparison → tooling → build system →
            debugging build system → rethink architecture → new idea → repeat
          </p>
          <p>And somewhere in there is a README saying:</p>
          <blockquote
            class="border-l-2 border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pl-5 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-800 dark:text-stone-200 not-italic"
          >
            TODO: implement core functionality
          </blockquote>
        </article>

        <footer
          class="border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 pt-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-500"
        >
          <p>
            Source: ChatGPT conversation, 7 September 2026. Posted here because it was too accurate
            not to keep.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a
              href="/for-fun"
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-stone-500 hover:text-stone-900 dark:hover:text-stone-100 transition-colors border-b border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-600 pb-0.5"
            >
              ← For fun
            </a>
          </p>
        </footer>
      </div>
    </main>

    <Footer client:load />
  </body>
</html>
